---
title: "Signals"
description: "Reactive component state through state, computed, and effect."
section: JavaScript
sectionOrder: 6
order: 2
---

Component scripts hold state in signals: reads are tracked, and writes notify what read them. `base/signals.js` wraps the TC39 Signals polyfill, and it is the only module in the kit that imports `signal-polyfill`; when engines ship native signals, one file changes.

```js
import { state, computed, effect } from "@zazz-ui/core/base/signals.js";
```

### state

A mutable value, read with `.get()` and written with `.set()`:

```js
const expanded = state(false);
expanded.get(); // false
expanded.set(true);
```

### computed

A derived value, cached until a dependency changes and recomputed lazily. Keep it pure; it may run at any time.

```js
const paused = computed(() => expanded.get() || hidden.get());
```

### effect

A tracked side effect for DOM writes. It runs immediately and again whenever a signal it read changes; re-runs are batched to a microtask, so ten writes in one handler trigger one re-run. The callback may return a cleanup, run before each re-run and on disposal.

```js
const dispose = effect(() => {
  node.dataset.paused = String(paused.get());
});

effect(() => render(count.get()), { signal }); // inside setup(signal): dies with the element
```

`effect` returns a disposer that also implements `Symbol.dispose`, so TypeScript sources can bind it with `using`.

## Division of labor

DOM events and observers are input adapters: they write into `state`. `computed` holds the pure derivations, which is what the unit tests cover. `effect` is the output adapter: it writes back to the DOM.

Timers, transition choreography, and DOM construction stay imperative, and the DOM itself remains the source of truth for element lists.
